From: "R. David Murray" <rdmurray@keene.edu>
Date: Mon, 11 Apr 1994 13:33:56 -0400 (EDT)
Subject: [#94-4-64] BUSI: MarketWare

I haven't been very interested in any of the current threads, so I 
thought I'd try to start a new one.

Intellectual property rights seems to be quite the hot topic right now in 
the wider world discovering the Internet.  We've gone several rounds on 
this topic on this list in the past, and I don't *intend* to restart that 
discussion.  My own conclusion about intellectual property rights is that 
they are not practical and should be junked wholesale.

Which begs the question: why write software?

The answer would seem to best be addressed by asking the question in an 
operational way: how can I make money from writing uncopywrited software?

I have no doubt there are lots of answers to that question.  Romana's 
ReputationWare is one answer.  (Don't make money off the software, make 
money off the personal advertising a good product buys you.)  I'd like to 
see if there is a way to make money from writing software using something 
analogous to the current way software often happens on the Net.

The process I'm thinking of is this:  someone pours some passion into a 
piece of software they want to see exist.  They release it to the Net.  
Other people send back bug reports and requests for enhancements.  Bug 
fixes and enhancements occur, often through the author folding in code 
developed by those who discovered the bug or wanted the enhancement.  A 
new official release is posted to the Net.  This cycle continues, 
sometimes with the central control passing from the original author to 
some new person, sometimes even with divergent versions comming into 
existence, which sometimes grow back together later and sometimes don't.

So, is it possible to set up to make a living from this scenario?  The 
difficulty, of course, is the perception that this kind of cooperation 
can only be achieved if all involved are volunteering their time (and 
there are psychological studies that give some weight to this perception).

So far, my partially developed idea goes like this:

Build an initial product.  Release it to the Net.  (While copyright is 
still theoretically enforced, you'll have to copyright it, of course, but 
make it freely copyable for any purpose, even commercial resale).

Now, make sure that you have a site that is always accessible where 
anyone can get a guaranteed original up-to-date copy of the program.  
Advertise this site *prominently* in all the documentation and all the 
postings you make regarding the software.

On this site, put up some WWW databases and forms for bug reports and
enhancement requests.  Here's where the money comes in.  When submitting a
bug report or enhancement request, the poster attaches a dollar amount
that they would pay for it, graded by time ($50 if done by tomorrow, $25
if done in a week, $5 if done in a month, etc).  Both lists must be
public, and parties other than the original submitter should be able to
attach their own bids.

Now you prioritize those lists by amount of effort vs potential income 
(recall that not all promises for payment will be kept), and work on the 
most valuable ones first.  (In advertising this point out that the 
advantage of this system is that those bugs will get fixed and those 
enhancements made that really are the most important, as measured by the 
dollars people are willing to spend on them.)

Improvements (IMO):

Track the payment patterns of people who submit requests and use it to 
weight their promises in future requests.

Make the bug/enhancement bids public, and take a percentage for folding
solutions into the base code.  This needs some work; there would have to
be some mechanism whereby potential contributors could "reserve" certain
bug fixes/enhancements as their exclusive "property"; once bid on, no one
else could supply a fix until some pre-set deadline had passed.  My
thoughts on this tend toward some sort of bidding system where
contributors would actually buy the rights to a bug or enhancement. 

Note that the success of this enterprise depends on your ability to 
maintain your code as the best possible version of the code, and to 
provide the bug/enhancement list service as the best possible service.
Otherwise somebody will rise up to compete with you starting from some 
older version of the code.

Comments?  Improvements?  Reasons why it would never work?

--David

