Subject: [#94-4-65] SCI-Faster than light

SCI:Faster than light
How to make a faster than light drive.

	For decades, since the development of the special theory of relativity,
	we as a civilization have been faced with a apparently hopeless dilemma.
	We know there are stars out there, we know they are very far away, and
	our cosmological circumstances have enforced upon us an unfair speed
	limit.
	
	To protest such a limitation I propose the following actions:
	1. Ignore it.
		People have been ignoring local speed limits for decades without
		any more trouble than a ticket, or if really careless, a well
		deserved smashup. By ignoring such a speed limit, we could improve
		the lot of mankind tremendously. I propose by starting with the
		local speed limits, since the speed of light wasn't defined as a
		limit until municipalities had their own universal laws regarding
		ebullient careening.
		
	2. Change it.
		I propose we make the speed of light something more easy to work
		with, and not such a useless and arbitrary number like 186282
		Miles/sec. If we made it, say, infinite, we could as a matter of
		course, go anywhere we like as long as adequate rest stops were
		provided. In addition, by upping the limit we would be able, once
		we became bored with the horde of stars in our galaxy, to visit
		other galaxies with more interesting scenery.
		
	3. Offer indulgences.
		Suppose the NBS might provide for indulgences like the sort the
		Catholic Church has been giving for centuries. If a wealthy patron
		were to offer the NBS a few million to exceed the speed of light
		on a one time basis, we would have both a very exciting commercial
		avenue and at the same time a very rich NBS, er... then again.
		Why don't you send it to me instead, since I'm less likely to spend
		it on insubstantials, and more likely to spend it on important
		things like my very own nuclear powered backyard barbecue.
	4. Forget it.
		Similar to 1, but more insidious and longer lasting. Excise from
		all books any mention of the limit, and recalculate all formulae
		to compensate for the hidden value. Sooner or later, the scientist
		will forget the speed of light all together and invent some very
		interesting products, like Maxwell's Perpetual Planet Crusher.
		(Used to get rid of those annoyingly useless little planets like
		Mercury which have inspired so many of our wars, and convert them
		into base gravel for the governments proposed version of the
		information super highway).
		
	5. Lie about it.
		Sooner or later everybody will know the truth, but until then
		everybody will believe you, much in the same way we are believing
		an inexperienced investor can plop down $1K and get back 100K on
		unsupervised trading. The prospects of a little fib regarding the
		speed of light makes my Newton just tingle with excitement and
		anticipation.
		
	6. Deny it.
		Have you ever wondered how the news media lately manages to report
		only half the news, when you have to go to the Singapore Daily to 
		get the other half? They do this by denying the facts for a more
		appetizing attenuation. I suggest we do the same thing with the
		speed limit. Instead of making it a hard fact we could just make
		it a suggestion. You know, like they do in our public schools,
		1+1=3 if it makes Joey feel better about himself. Since I've taken
		umbrage about the unfair nature of the speed of light, I am announcing
		here and now that I would like to apply for special status and get
		a variance due to my restricting cosmological handicap. I imagine,
		by the time a third of our population is labeled under such a
		protestation, the law will be revoke entirely for the more palatable
		suggestion.
		
		
	To actually make the drive, you do the above. Use allot of fuel in a
	standard rocket motor, and cancel any short term plans. Oh.. I just
	remembered, leave you key in the burglar position, you know, just
	under the door mat, in case you get fined for speeding.
	
	Anderson Vogue
	




