From: davisd@nimitz.ee.washington.edu
Date: Mon, 11 Apr 94 16:38:04 -0700
Subject: [#94-4-67] Land vs Other Property 

> From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
> Subject: Land vs Other Property
> 
> Why should ...

Steve asks many "why shoulds" with respect to property. Let me
differentiate between two kinds of why shoulds: why should there be,
and why should I. 

In both of these cases, should is being used in the sense of
obligation. Since I am not big on moral duty, if you believe in such
things, you'll have to get an answer from someone else. 

"Why should there be" type of questions leave the actor with the
supposed obligation entirely unspecified, and so are at best unclear.
(I will note that I stupidly said "there should be" in one of my posts
on this subject. Bad habits die hard.)

As for "why should I" questions, you would have to explain to me your
theory of moral duty for me to give you an answer, since my theory of
moral duty is that it is religious hooey.

On the other hand, if we take "should" merely to indicate that an
action is consistent with your chosen values, then I could answer your
questions by showing how the indicated actions will best fulfill your
values. 

With respect to property rights, however, its not quite so simple,
since your wishes are not the only ones involved. Everyone else has
wishes as well, and you must take their wishes into account in
choosing your own actions. Everyone else's wishes (and power) are part
of the context in which you make your decisions.

When I made my original claims, I conditioned my statements on an
assumption of reasonable people. We can probably agree that the world
is not full of reasonable people. We can still discuss my claim,
however, since I consider some of you reasonable people, and since
property rights are about the relationships between people. We can
discuss what kind of property relationships we would accept, given
some context of relative power, desire, and wealth of the
participants.

I'll just comment on a few of Steve's questions.

> Why should people
> be able to keep *anything* without compensating me?

Are you just asking to be convinced that you should respect property
rights? If so, you'll have to specify the context you had in mind. If
it is the context of this planet, this day, I would say police guns
are a good reason to start.

> If you think there should be some sort of equality between your/our 
> wishes and other people's, what the heck for?  Does it have something to
> do with workability or getting other people to agree to it?  If so, aren't
> you bowing down before their ability to coerce you?

Dealing with people in such a way that you take into account the power
they have to harm you strikes me as quite rational. If you consider
that "bowing down", make the most of it. 

> Suppose my respect for others' claims to property (both in land and other
> stuff) has to do with their eventually, overall, probably doing something
> worthwhile with it that makes my world a better place.  Isn't that just as
> base a motivation as wanting to avoid their coercion?

What do you mean by base? Again, it seems perfectly reasonable to me.

> Why do you have a right to an opinion on the matter?

I never said I had any such right. I don't check for rights before I
form opinions.

