From: davisd@nimitz.ee.washington.edu
Date: Mon, 11 Apr 94 17:10:39 -0700
Subject: [#94-4-68] land property 

> From: dasher@netcom.com (Anton Sherwood)
> Subject: land property 
> 
> Is this just a demand for justification of _original_ property
> in land (how nonproperty becomes property), or for justification
> of _keeping_ property while landless people continue to be born?

Property rights are about the relationships between people. Any
justifications should justify all relationships one is making a claim
about. If landless folks are born, and you want the original property
rights to continue with those people, you should justify that
relationship as well.

By the way, I find the original claim of homesteaders quite
compelling, and would consider them exempt from a land tax for a
period of years.

> One reason for the landless to respect property is that everyone
> is a potential property owner; respecting another's property now
> improves the chances that when you acquire property it will in
> turn be respected.  

This does weigh toward respecting property in land. It does not tip
the balance, in my opinion. I'll just note that the same argument
could be used with respect to slavery, as long as a slave has the
possibility of becoming a master. Would it convince you?

> I see little difference between your asking
> me to respect your land property and my asking a child not to
> touch my books (a few of which are older than my grandparents).

I see a lot of diferences, some of them relevant to your property
rights, and some of them irrelevant to your property rights.
 
> If land belongs to everyone in the cosmos as someone suggested,

Not me. 

> should we be putting some (most?) of this rent in escrow for
> people whose existence we predict but whom we have not yet found?

I don't think so.

> -- but how do we justify
> _any_ rights without appealing either to mysticism (rights are
> an essential human quality) or to pragmatism (rights lead to a
> stable functional society)?  

I probably won't justify rights.

We can come to an agreement over property rights, in the proper
context, by bargaining with each other over how we will treat each
other. My suggestion is that reasonable people would come to roughly
the kind of system I have described. 

To answer questions about particular property rights, we need a
general discussion on property rights and the reasons people should
respect them. I have my own theories, but I have seen no reasoned
defense of property in land, save claims that land is no different
than other kinds of property. Clearly land is in some ways different
from cars and boats. If it is the same in the aspects *relevant* to
property rights, you should first establish what those relevant
aspects are, and then show that land is indeed the same in those
respects.

