From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Date: Mon, 11 Apr 1994 21:55:16 -0400
Subject: [#94-4-70] RAPTOR: A trojan horse of anarchocapitalism ?

   Apogee and Cygnus Software just released a new shareware game, RAPTOR.
You are a pilot  and fly over a terrain with lots of tough stuff on  and
above it, which you have to shoot - and, occasionally, pick - up.
   Apart from elevating the shoot-them-up to a new level of graphical and
sound quality,  it has some  distinctly  extropian features:  the usually
useless game points here turn into a valuable currency that you can  earn
fulfilling your mission and spend buying ammunition/health/protection.
You can also sell any of your possessions back to the store.
Unfortunately, the store has a natural monopoly and the prices are fixed.
  Your wealth, health and power  smoothly turn into each other during the
game, with temporary disbalances  (you can't shop while flying)  that you
have to watch, weighing tactical opportunities against temporary resource
bottlenecks and strategic goals.
 
  A multi-player extension of RAPTOR, with a free ammunition market, would
be a great training tool for any anarchocapitalist space cadet.  ;-)
 
  I wonder how it would compete with a statist game.
Maybe if somebody could use a newly built [and yet uninfested by statists]
world to demonstrate that anarchocapitalism can suggest clear organizational
advantages and be fun to live in,  the users would be more receptive to the
idea that a setup like this could well work in wherever they come from...

